  "correct_answer": "B. Operate within 36 hours (day of or day after admission) once optimised",
  "why_correct": "Hip fracture in a fit patient should be surgically fixed early — within about 36 hours / on the day of or the day after admission — once medically optimised, because delay increases complications, pain and mortality. Comprehensive geriatric care (orthogeriatrics) supports recovery.",
  "why_wrong": "A. Age alone is not a reason to withhold surgery. C. A 2-week delay worsens outcomes. D. Conservative management causes immobility complications. E. Surgery is the standard treatment, not contingent on losing mobility.",
  "pearl": "Hip fracture → early surgery (≤~36 h) once optimised + orthogeriatric care.",
  "thinking": "Stable hip fracture ↓ early fixation ↓ surgery within ~36 hours",
  "exam_trap": "Delaying hip-fracture surgery or treating it conservatively — early fixation reduces mortality.",
  "takeaway": "Hip fracture is fixed surgically within about 36 hours once the patient is optimised.",
