  "correct_letter": "B",
  "correct_answer": "B. Split/remove the cast and urgently assess compartment pressures",
  "why_correct": "Pain out of proportion and pain on passive stretch are the earliest signs of compartment syndrome; pulselessness is a late, ominous sign, so present pulses do NOT exclude it. The cast must be split/removed to relieve pressure and compartment pressures assessed urgently, with fasciotomy if confirmed.",
  "why_wrong": "A. An outpatient study is far too slow. C. Present pulses do not exclude compartment syndrome. D. Delay risks irreversible muscle/nerve damage. E. Elevation to heart level only (not above) and observation alone are inadequate once suspected.",
  "pearl": "Pain out of proportion + pain on passive stretch = compartment syndrome; pulses are a LATE sign.",
  "thinking": "Cast + pain out of proportion + pain on passive stretch ↓ compartment syndrome ↓ split cast + measure pressures",
  "exam_trap": "Being reassured by present pulses — pulselessness is late, and waiting for it loses the limb.",
  "takeaway": "Compartment syndrome is diagnosed on pain out of proportion and on passive stretch — not by absent pulses.",
